[38;2;124;124;124mwhere Λ has wandering points that are not in the closure of periodic points.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mClassic example: Consider a horseshoe-type construction but where the hyperbolic[0m[38;2;124;124;124mset includes wandering orbits.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the simplest example: Take a diffeomorphism with a hyperbolic fixed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpoint p. Consider a point q whose orbit is homoclinic to p (i.e., q is in the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstable manifold of p and also... no, a homoclinic point). The closure of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124morbit of q together with p forms a hyperbolic set. But this set is {p} ∪ {f^n(q)[0m[38;2;124;124;124m: n ∈ Z}. The only periodic point is p. Is q in the closure of periodic points? [0m[38;2;124;124;124mThe orbit of q accumulates on p, so p is in the closure. But q itself: is q a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlimit of periodic points? In this set, the only periodic point is p, and q ≠ p. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo periodic points = {p}, which is not dense in {p} ∪ {f^n(q)} (since q is not [0m
[38;2;124;124;124min the closure of {p}... well, the closure of {p} is {p}, and q ∉ {p}).[0m

